WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.280
Imagine you're sitting at your kitchen table.

00:03.460 --> 00:03.660
Right.

00:03.800 --> 00:09.720
It's late, the house is completely quiet, and you are trying to solve a jigsaw puzzle.

00:10.760 --> 00:11.520
You know the feeling.

00:11.640 --> 00:12.400
Oh, absolutely.

00:13.020 --> 00:14.420
The late night puzzle grind.

00:14.480 --> 00:15.080
Yeah, exactly.

00:15.200 --> 00:16.400
You've been at this for hours.

00:16.520 --> 00:18.680
You have the edges all neatly snapped together.

00:19.100 --> 00:24.500
You know, you've got that satisfying, undeniable border done, and now you're working on the interior.

00:24.740 --> 00:25.400
The hard part.

00:25.520 --> 00:26.400
Right, the hard part.

00:26.660 --> 00:29.120
But you're hitting a massive wall.

00:29.120 --> 00:33.920
I mean, no matter how you arrange the pieces in front of you, they just do not fit.

00:34.160 --> 00:34.440
Right, yeah.

00:34.660 --> 00:36.240
You try turning them 90 degrees.

00:36.580 --> 00:41.800
You try forcing them just a little bit, seeing if maybe the cardboard is just warped.

00:42.020 --> 00:47.640
You even take a step back and check your work, rubbing your eyes, starting to doubt your own vision.

00:47.960 --> 00:49.320
You think you're just looking at it wrong.

00:49.500 --> 00:49.900
Exactly.

00:50.340 --> 00:51.900
You walk over to the wall switch.

00:52.100 --> 00:55.380
You turn on the blinding overhead light just to gather more visibility.

00:55.380 --> 01:00.420
And you're standing there telling yourself, you know, I must be making a mistake in how

01:00.420 --> 01:01.960
I'm perceiving these shapes.

01:02.160 --> 01:04.300
I'm just predicting the pattern wrong.

01:04.420 --> 01:05.800
Which is a logical conclusion.

01:06.140 --> 01:06.600
It is.

01:06.860 --> 01:10.740
But what if the problem isn't how you are solving the puzzle?

01:11.300 --> 01:16.160
What if the problem is that someone, while you were out of the room, slipped a handful

01:16.160 --> 01:19.380
of pieces from a totally different game into your box?

01:19.820 --> 01:20.560
Wow, yeah.

01:20.780 --> 01:25.800
I mean, you could spend an entire lifetime tweeting your strategy, you know, adjusting

01:25.800 --> 01:29.760
your vision, second guessing your logic, and you would never ever solve it because those

01:29.760 --> 01:31.500
pieces shouldn't be on the table in the first place.

01:31.640 --> 01:33.440
They don't belong to the reality of the puzzle.

01:33.440 --> 01:40.500
That distinction between a mistake in your strategy and a fundamental contamination of your

01:40.500 --> 01:43.800
game board, that's really the heart of what we're exploring today.

01:43.800 --> 01:44.280
Right.

01:44.400 --> 01:49.360
We're looking at the critical difference between making an error within the rules of

01:49.360 --> 01:54.500
a system and realizing that the boundaries of the system itself are just completely wrong.

01:54.600 --> 01:54.820
Yeah.

01:55.300 --> 02:00.720
And I mean, when you zoom out to a world characterized by absolute information overload, understanding

02:00.720 --> 02:05.280
how we revise our beliefs and understanding the hidden machinery we use to rewrite our entire

02:05.280 --> 02:09.720
menu of possibilities, it becomes just as vital as the actual beliefs we hold.

02:10.020 --> 02:10.780
It really does.

02:10.780 --> 02:16.120
So today's deep dive is basically going to act as our microscope for this problem.

02:16.800 --> 02:22.960
Our mission today is to explore an incredibly thought-provoking paper from August, 2026.

02:23.180 --> 02:25.740
It's by an independent researcher known as Flyxion.

02:26.100 --> 02:27.580
It's a fascinating piece of work.

02:27.660 --> 02:28.260
It really is.

02:28.300 --> 02:34.840
The paper is titled Beyond Prediction Error, Warrant, Repair, and Refusal in Predictive Architectures.

02:34.840 --> 02:41.420
And I'll be honest, it is dense, it's mathematical, and frankly, it's a fascinating look into the

02:41.420 --> 02:43.980
cognitive architecture of complex systems.

02:44.440 --> 02:44.700
Right.

02:44.860 --> 02:49.500
And when we say complex systems here, we're talking about human brains, advanced artificial

02:49.500 --> 02:50.180
intelligence.

02:50.360 --> 02:54.760
Basically, any information processing system that has to figure out what is real and what

02:54.760 --> 02:55.700
is just noise.

02:55.840 --> 02:56.200
Exactly.

02:56.200 --> 02:58.180
So we're going to track a core theme today.

02:58.560 --> 03:03.460
When these systems encounter a glaring error, do they simply tweak the probabilities of what

03:03.460 --> 03:04.700
they already believe to be true?

03:05.020 --> 03:09.380
Or, you know, do they possess a fundamentally different, completely separate mechanism to

03:09.380 --> 03:12.220
just scrap and rewrite their entire menu of possibilities?

03:12.740 --> 03:16.900
And to really set the stage for why this is such a disruptive question, we have to look

03:16.900 --> 03:19.880
at the reigning paradigm in cognitive science and AI right now.

03:19.980 --> 03:20.200
Right.

03:20.280 --> 03:21.360
The establishment view.

03:21.360 --> 03:28.400
Because for decades, the dominant framework has been something called predictive coding.

03:28.940 --> 03:32.660
It essentially views the brain as a relentless prediction machine.

03:33.160 --> 03:33.220
Okay.

03:33.760 --> 03:38.460
And Flyxion is stepping into this incredibly well-established arena and suggesting that we

03:38.460 --> 03:41.600
might be conflating two completely different types of learning.

03:41.980 --> 03:48.540
If that argument holds up, I mean, the implications for how we understand human stubbornness, scientific

03:48.540 --> 03:52.420
breakthroughs, artificial reasoning, it's profound.

03:52.640 --> 03:52.780
Yeah.

03:52.860 --> 03:53.820
It's a massive shift.

03:54.040 --> 03:54.120
Yeah.

03:54.200 --> 03:58.680
So I want to make sure you, listening to this, are fully grounded in that reigning paradigm

03:58.680 --> 03:59.900
before we start tearing it apart.

03:59.940 --> 04:00.340
Good idea.

04:00.560 --> 04:00.700
Right.

04:00.760 --> 04:06.240
Because to really understand how a mind changes, we first need to examine how we currently think

04:06.240 --> 04:06.700
it works.

04:06.960 --> 04:09.040
Let's do a deep dive into predictive coding 101.

04:09.300 --> 04:09.480
Sure.

04:09.760 --> 04:12.440
Let's wind the clock back slightly to understand the philosophical shift.

04:12.860 --> 04:17.760
So historically, the intuitive way to think about perception was as a bottom-up process.

04:17.760 --> 04:19.620
Meaning starting from the raw data.

04:20.000 --> 04:22.000
It was viewed as passive transduction.

04:22.360 --> 04:27.420
So light bounces off a coffee cup, it hits your retina, the signal travels up your optic

04:27.420 --> 04:32.320
nerve to the visual cortex, and your brain kind of pieces together those raw pixels into

04:32.320 --> 04:33.240
the image of a cup.

04:33.320 --> 04:34.680
Like a camera taking a picture.

04:34.800 --> 04:35.820
Exactly like a camera.

04:36.280 --> 04:41.880
But predictive coding, which was championed by researchers like Rajesh Rao and Dana Ballard

04:41.880 --> 04:48.040
in the late 90s, and then massively expanded by Carl Fristen, it flips that entire sequence

04:48.040 --> 04:48.600
on its head.

04:48.900 --> 04:49.060
Okay.

04:49.160 --> 04:49.620
How so?

04:49.900 --> 04:52.700
It argues the brain is a top-down inference engine.

04:53.160 --> 04:57.080
Meaning the brain isn't just waiting for the picture to arrive, it's already painting it.

04:57.140 --> 04:57.400
Yes.

04:57.760 --> 05:02.800
It is continually predicting the causes of the sensory signals before the signals even arrive.

05:02.800 --> 05:07.040
Your brain generates a model of a coffee cup and projects it downward.

05:07.540 --> 05:12.140
The incoming sensory data from your eyes is really just there to check the brain's work.

05:12.340 --> 05:16.620
That is, I mean, it's like your brain is always one step ahead, essentially hallucinating

05:16.620 --> 05:21.240
reality, and then just using your eyes and ears to proofread the hallucination.

05:21.440 --> 05:22.600
That's a really good way to put it.

05:22.600 --> 05:25.160
And if there's a typo, it fixes it.

05:25.720 --> 05:30.640
But I guess my question is, why would biology evolve to do it backward like that?

05:31.100 --> 05:35.800
It seems like it would take way more energy to guess reality than to just, you know, look

05:35.800 --> 05:36.180
at it.

05:36.400 --> 05:39.060
It actually solves a massive efficiency problem.

05:39.420 --> 05:42.180
The world is just incredibly noisy and ambiguous.

05:43.020 --> 05:47.400
I mean, if your brain had to process every single photon of light in every sound wave from

05:47.400 --> 05:49.880
scratch, every single millisecond...

05:49.880 --> 05:50.700
To just freeze up.

05:50.700 --> 05:51.080
Exactly.

05:51.460 --> 05:53.700
You'd be paralyzed by computational overload.

05:54.560 --> 05:59.540
So by predicting what's going to happen next, based on past experience, the brain only

05:59.540 --> 06:02.600
has to process the differences between its guess and reality.

06:02.840 --> 06:03.500
Okay, I see.

06:03.640 --> 06:05.060
It only processes surprises.

06:05.280 --> 06:06.340
Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

06:06.420 --> 06:09.580
It's saving bandwidth by only caring about the unexpected stuff.

06:09.940 --> 06:13.080
So how does this actually work mechanically, like in the theory?

06:13.260 --> 06:18.520
Well, the formalization of predictive coding outlines a very specific, continuous, five-stage

06:18.520 --> 06:18.940
pipeline.

06:19.040 --> 06:19.980
Okay, five stages.

06:19.980 --> 06:20.300
Right.

06:20.480 --> 06:21.380
The first stage is prediction.

06:21.760 --> 06:24.740
The system generates a top-down expectation of what is about to happen.

06:24.860 --> 06:26.520
The second stage is comparison.

06:27.520 --> 06:32.520
The system receives the actual incoming sensory data and compares it to the prediction.

06:32.760 --> 06:34.380
So hallucination meets reality.

06:34.720 --> 06:35.160
Exactly.

06:35.740 --> 06:37.240
Then the third stage is error.

06:37.920 --> 06:43.200
If there's a discrepancy, if the hallucination doesn't match the raw data, an error signal is

06:43.200 --> 06:43.640
generated.

06:43.640 --> 06:47.780
Okay, so if I predict my coffee cup is full, but I pick it up and it's light and empty,

06:47.780 --> 06:50.480
that massive surprise is the error signal.

06:50.600 --> 06:50.800
Yes.

06:51.000 --> 06:54.280
But then we hit the fourth stage, which is incredibly crucial.

06:54.600 --> 06:56.060
This is precision weighting.

06:56.460 --> 06:57.600
Precision weighting.

06:57.600 --> 06:57.880
Right.

06:58.000 --> 07:01.140
The brain doesn't just blindly accept every single error signal.

07:01.420 --> 07:04.240
It evaluates how much it should trust that error.

07:04.360 --> 07:05.000
Oh, interesting.

07:05.460 --> 07:05.660
Yeah.

07:05.740 --> 07:07.440
Like, is the environment dark and noisy?

07:07.860 --> 07:08.500
Are you tired?

07:09.500 --> 07:14.440
If the data is unreliable, the system assigns a low precision weight to the error, effectively

07:14.440 --> 07:15.160
ignoring it.

07:15.320 --> 07:18.780
If the data is clear and reliable, it assigns a high precision weight.

07:18.780 --> 07:19.160
I see.

07:19.220 --> 07:21.480
It's like a, it's a volume knob for the surprise.

07:22.160 --> 07:22.640
Exactly.

07:22.800 --> 07:23.600
A volume knob.

07:23.880 --> 07:27.280
And that leads to the final fifth stage, which is model update.

07:28.320 --> 07:33.940
Based on that weighted error signal, the system adjusts its internal model so it can make a

07:33.940 --> 07:36.420
better, more accurate prediction the next time around.

07:36.420 --> 07:37.280
So that's the loop.

07:37.540 --> 07:41.400
Prediction, comparison, error, precision weighting, and model update.

07:41.800 --> 07:42.580
That's the loop.

07:42.720 --> 07:45.420
It is an elegant, incredibly robust framework.

07:45.480 --> 07:46.160
It is elegant.

07:46.160 --> 07:51.400
But then we introduce Flyxion's challenger framework, because in the paper, Flyxion

07:51.400 --> 07:57.160
introduces a model built on the concepts of admissibility and repair, and it operates

07:57.160 --> 07:59.060
on what looks like a totally parallel track.

07:59.580 --> 08:01.680
Let me walk through the pipeline Flyxion outlines.

08:02.040 --> 08:03.940
So first, the system makes a commitment.

08:04.360 --> 08:05.540
Second, it encounters a witness.

08:06.300 --> 08:07.800
Third, there is a mismatch.

08:08.120 --> 08:08.960
Fourth, there is a warrant.

08:09.540 --> 08:13.760
And fifth, an outcome drawn from a set of options repair, refusal, or continuation.

08:13.760 --> 08:18.580
And looking at those two pipelines side by side, that is exactly where the academic friction

08:18.580 --> 08:19.960
in this paper begins.

08:20.120 --> 08:23.320
Well, let me play the skeptic for a moment here, because I have to admit, when I read

08:23.320 --> 08:27.020
those two pipelines in the source material, I felt like I was just reading a thesaurus.

08:27.240 --> 08:27.700
Oh, for sure.

08:27.920 --> 08:30.600
I mean, prediction sounds exactly like commitment.

08:31.640 --> 08:33.820
Comparison is just another word for observing a witness.

08:34.560 --> 08:37.000
And an error sounds identically like a mismatch.

08:37.200 --> 08:38.800
They map onto each other very closely.

08:38.800 --> 08:39.240
Right.

08:39.580 --> 08:44.220
Even the fourth step precision weighting versus warrant, it just sounds like two different

08:44.220 --> 08:49.000
groups of scientists trying to describe the exact same process of deciding if a discrepancy

08:49.000 --> 08:49.380
matters.

08:50.160 --> 08:53.380
Are we sure this isn't just like a turf war over vocabulary?

08:53.740 --> 08:55.800
That skepticism is entirely warranted.

08:56.460 --> 08:57.140
No pun intended.

08:57.240 --> 08:57.500
Right.

08:57.840 --> 09:00.460
Flyxion actually anticipates that exact reaction.

09:00.580 --> 09:00.840
Right.

09:01.240 --> 09:04.120
The resemblance between the two frameworks is undeniable.

09:04.120 --> 09:09.600
And the paper goes out of its way to establish that there is a massive amount of genuine foundational

09:09.600 --> 09:13.040
agreement between them before it ever introduces the divergence.

09:13.460 --> 09:16.180
OK, I think we need to map out where they agree first, then.

09:16.280 --> 09:19.540
It'll help clarify what is actually at stake when they finally disagree.

09:19.740 --> 09:20.480
Yeah, let's do that.

09:20.560 --> 09:25.920
So the foundational agreement is that both frameworks entirely reject that old, passive,

09:26.280 --> 09:27.680
bottom-up model of perception.

09:28.080 --> 09:28.360
Right.

09:28.420 --> 09:31.760
They both agree the system is doing active, top-down work.

09:32.100 --> 09:32.460
Yes.

09:32.460 --> 09:36.480
Furthermore, both models are deeply hierarchical.

09:36.700 --> 09:37.680
What does that mean in practice?

09:37.940 --> 09:38.940
Like, hierarchical?

09:39.080 --> 09:42.500
It means the brain isn't just making one big guess about the world.

09:43.140 --> 09:45.160
It's a nested structure of guesses.

09:45.400 --> 09:45.560
Correct.

09:46.180 --> 09:49.260
Lower-level visual circuits are predicting the angles of lines.

09:49.980 --> 09:53.540
Mid-level circuits are predicting shapes based on those lines.

09:54.100 --> 09:57.800
High-level circuits are predicting a coffee cup based on those shapes.

09:57.880 --> 09:58.440
Oh, I see.

09:58.440 --> 10:04.800
So in both predictive coding and Flyxion's admissibility framework, lower-level sensory discrepancies

10:04.800 --> 10:07.100
are constrained by higher-level expectations.

10:07.620 --> 10:12.400
This is actually a lineage of thought that traces all the way back to the 19th century physicist

10:12.400 --> 10:13.800
Hermann von Helmholtz.

10:13.900 --> 10:14.680
Oh, wow.

10:14.680 --> 10:14.940
Yeah.

10:15.120 --> 10:18.420
He first proposed the idea of perception as unconscious inference.

10:18.600 --> 10:21.900
Okay, so they share the Helmholtz DNA, a hierarchy of guessing and checking.

10:22.020 --> 10:22.340
They do.

10:22.540 --> 10:26.620
And they also seamlessly handle a concept known as active inference.

10:26.740 --> 10:29.540
Oh, I want to pause on active inference because it's a huge concept.

10:30.140 --> 10:33.180
This is the idea that you don't just sit there changing your mind to fit the world.

10:33.240 --> 10:37.120
You actually move your body to change the world to fit your mind.

10:37.120 --> 10:39.180
That is the absolute essence of it, yeah.

10:39.660 --> 10:45.080
If your internal model is strongly predicting that you are in a warm room, but your sensory

10:45.080 --> 10:48.100
data says you are cold, you have a mismatch.

10:48.280 --> 10:48.540
Right.

10:48.780 --> 10:53.460
Now, you can resolve that by updating your model to accept that the room is actually cold.

10:53.960 --> 10:55.960
Or you can perform active inference.

10:56.400 --> 10:59.240
You get up, you walk across the room, and you turn up the thermostat.

10:59.560 --> 11:04.560
So you change the physical environment so the incoming sensory data aligns with your original

11:04.560 --> 11:05.500
prediction of warmth.

11:05.500 --> 11:06.060
Exactly.

11:07.060 --> 11:12.920
And both predictive coding and Flyxion agree that this kind of physical intervention in

11:12.920 --> 11:17.980
the world is a core, legitimate way that systems minimize error.

11:18.120 --> 11:20.280
Okay, so they are standing on the same foundation.

11:20.560 --> 11:22.060
They both believe in top-down guessing.

11:22.320 --> 11:24.020
They both believe in a hierarchy of circuits.

11:24.300 --> 11:27.400
And they both believe in taking physical action to fix errors.

11:27.660 --> 11:29.240
Yes, they absolutely do.

11:29.460 --> 11:35.220
But that cozy resemblance shatters when we ask one specific, almost dangerous question about

11:35.220 --> 11:36.340
how these systems function.

11:36.440 --> 11:40.580
The question of what the system is actually allowed to revise, meaning, you know, when an

11:40.580 --> 11:45.120
error happens and a change needs to be made, what parts of the internal machinery are open

11:45.120 --> 11:45.640
for editing?

11:46.000 --> 11:47.200
That is the dividing line.

11:47.200 --> 11:52.100
The frameworks are not identical because they have fundamentally different answers to that

11:52.100 --> 11:52.840
exact question.

11:53.560 --> 11:56.640
And to see the difference, plain English isn't really going to cut it.

11:57.660 --> 12:02.560
We have to look under the hood at the specific mathematical operators that drive the inference.

12:02.760 --> 12:08.560
We have to move from like the philosophy of perception into the hard mechanics of how a system defines its

12:08.560 --> 12:09.160
own reality.

12:09.880 --> 12:11.140
Let's pull out the blueprints, then.

12:11.400 --> 12:13.200
Let's draw the boundaries of belief.

12:13.940 --> 12:18.300
To map this out, Flyxion introduces a taxonomy of mathematical operators.

12:18.840 --> 12:21.360
First, we have to define the playing field itself.

12:21.540 --> 12:22.300
Right, the space.

12:22.460 --> 12:27.320
The paper uses the Greek letter omega to represent the space of admissible hypotheses.

12:28.020 --> 12:33.080
For you listening, think of omega as the current menu of things the system believes are even possible.

12:33.200 --> 12:35.040
It's the absolute sandbox of reality.

12:35.040 --> 12:38.460
If something isn't in omega, the system cannot think about it.

12:38.460 --> 12:41.020
Omega is your universe of live options.

12:41.620 --> 12:45.580
And Flyxion defines three distinct mathematical operators that interact with this space.

12:45.720 --> 12:46.600
Okay, let's go through them.

12:46.840 --> 12:50.940
The first is operator U. This represents a change of position within the space.

12:51.720 --> 12:57.200
Mathematically, it is typed as a function that takes an observation and the current space

12:57.200 --> 13:00.120
and maps to a new point within that same space.

13:00.180 --> 13:03.860
Okay, let's translate that into a physical scenario so I don't lose the thread here.

13:03.860 --> 13:05.100
Let's use a restaurant.

13:05.100 --> 13:10.760
Okay, operator U is me sitting at a table looking at the physical menu and deciding to change

13:10.760 --> 13:12.900
my order from a burger to a salad.

13:13.320 --> 13:13.600
Right.

13:13.600 --> 13:17.880
I am just shifting my commitment between items that are already printed on the page.

13:17.960 --> 13:19.060
I haven't changed the restaurant.

13:19.220 --> 13:20.480
I've just changed my selection.

13:20.480 --> 13:21.780
That captures it perfectly.

13:22.320 --> 13:25.040
Operator U is inference within the given rules.

13:25.600 --> 13:27.600
Now, the second operator is operator Q.

13:27.800 --> 13:28.280
Q, correct.

13:28.640 --> 13:31.140
This represents a change in the evidential state.

13:32.240 --> 13:36.680
Mathematically, it maps observations in the space to a new set of evidence.

13:37.040 --> 13:39.740
This relates to that active inference we just discussed.

13:40.220 --> 13:43.760
It is the system going out into the world and actively gathering new data.

13:43.760 --> 13:49.560
Now, sticking with the restaurant, is operator Q me calling the waiter over and asking,

13:49.960 --> 13:51.260
hey, how is the salad made?

13:51.320 --> 13:52.480
Is the dressing on the side?

13:53.020 --> 13:56.940
I am gathering more evidence about the items on the menu to help me make my you decision.

13:57.140 --> 13:57.280
Yes.

13:57.400 --> 14:02.520
But the menu itself, my omega, is still exactly the same piece of laminated paper.

14:02.660 --> 14:03.780
That's a great way to look at it.

14:04.000 --> 14:09.660
Operator Q changes the pool of evidence, which informs your selection, but it does not change

14:09.660 --> 14:11.120
the menu of possibilities.

14:11.120 --> 14:13.740
Now, this brings us to the critical divergence.

14:14.420 --> 14:16.460
The third operator is operator A.

14:17.060 --> 14:20.460
This operator is fundamentally mathematically different from the other two.

14:20.680 --> 14:21.420
Okay, operator A.

14:21.420 --> 14:26.540
Operator A maps a class of admissibility spaces to a new class of admissibility spaces.

14:26.900 --> 14:30.140
It takes omega and maps it to a new omega prime.

14:30.240 --> 14:32.120
So it maps a space to a space.

14:32.160 --> 14:32.640
Exactly.

14:33.200 --> 14:35.320
It changes the actual boundaries of the menu itself.

14:35.680 --> 14:38.540
It extends the menu by adding concepts that didn't exist,

14:38.540 --> 14:42.060
or it contracts the menu by completely eliminating concepts.

14:42.380 --> 14:46.140
Okay, so if U is picking a burger and Q is asking about the salad,

14:46.800 --> 14:51.520
operator A is the restaurant manager marching over to my table with a thick black sharpie,

14:52.140 --> 14:55.220
crossing out the burger completely so nobody can ever order it again,

14:55.340 --> 15:01.400
or suddenly pulling a secret menu out of his apron with items that literally did not exist

15:01.400 --> 15:03.780
in my universe of choices five minutes ago.

15:03.780 --> 15:08.080
Let's run with that analogy because it highlights the core mechanical difference perfectly.

15:08.760 --> 15:13.800
The manager crossing out the item alters what is allowed to exist in your reality.

15:14.380 --> 15:19.620
You can no longer exercise operator U to select the burger because the burger is no longer in omega.

15:19.800 --> 15:20.620
It's just gone.

15:20.720 --> 15:21.160
It's gone.

