29:47.060 --> 29:49.560
The paper brings up Bayesian structure learning.

29:50.140 --> 29:59.600
Specifically, it mentions models with amazing, slightly confusing names, like the Chinese restaurant process and the Indian buffet process.

29:59.600 --> 30:05.300
Now, to a casual listener, these sound like lunch plans, but they are actually highly complex algorithms.

30:05.640 --> 30:06.140
They are, yeah.

30:06.260 --> 30:10.680
I want to make sure we actually explain how these work before we let Flyxion tear them down.

30:10.900 --> 30:13.640
Can you explain what a Chinese restaurant process is doing?

30:13.860 --> 30:16.600
Let's break it down, because it is an ingenious piece of math.

30:17.220 --> 30:23.080
The Chinese restaurant process is a non-parametric Bayesian model used in machine learning.

30:23.080 --> 30:30.720
It models how a system can cluster data into categories when it doesn't know in advance how many categories there should be.

30:31.320 --> 30:33.760
So imagine a restaurant with an infinite number of tables.

30:33.980 --> 30:34.660
Right, infinite tables.

30:34.860 --> 30:35.720
Customer walks in.

30:36.040 --> 30:37.840
This customer represents a new piece of data.

30:38.080 --> 30:39.180
They sit at the first table.

30:39.640 --> 30:40.940
Now, a second customer walks in.

30:41.340 --> 30:46.340
They can either sit at the first table with the first customer, or they can choose to sit at a brand new empty table.

30:46.560 --> 30:51.720
The probability of them sitting at an occupied table is proportional to how many people are already sitting there.

30:51.720 --> 30:53.720
So the crowded tables attract more people.

30:53.860 --> 30:55.820
It's like a popularity contest for data.

30:56.020 --> 30:56.460
Exactly.

30:57.040 --> 31:03.240
But there is always a calculated non-zero probability that a customer will decide to start a new table.

31:03.900 --> 31:13.400
As more and more customers enter, as the system processes more and more data, it naturally forms clusters at different tables, and it continuously opens new tables as needed.

31:13.400 --> 31:21.920
So the critic looks at this algorithm and says to Flyxion, look, the system is growing its latent categories as new data arrives.

31:22.020 --> 31:23.060
It's adding new tables.

31:23.260 --> 31:25.860
