31:25.920 --> 31:27.420
It's expanding the space.

31:27.420 --> 31:33.380
It is a formidable objection, because at first glance, opening a new table looks exactly like domain revision.

31:33.620 --> 31:36.820
But Flyxion's rebuttal exposes a fatal flaw in the comparison.

31:37.800 --> 31:44.880
The Chinese restaurant process models domain growth, yes, but that growth is drawn from a prior overgrowth that was fixed in advance by the programmer.

31:45.240 --> 31:47.660
What does a prior overgrowth mean in plain English?

31:48.020 --> 31:56.260
It means the mathematical rule that dictates exactly how and when a new table could be opened was already baked into the system's code from the very beginning.

31:56.260 --> 32:00.640
The system is just pulling from a predetermined menu of possible enlargements.

32:01.120 --> 32:05.500
It fundamentally lacks the warrant condition that defines true domain revision.

32:05.820 --> 32:08.560
The warrant being a genuine, unpredicted surprise.

32:08.880 --> 32:09.140
Yes.

32:09.700 --> 32:15.780
In the Chinese restaurant process, the system is not rewriting its boundaries in response to an unthinkable anomaly.

32:16.340 --> 32:23.260
It is just executing a highly sophisticated version of precision weighting operator P operating one metal level up.

32:23.460 --> 32:24.640
Ah, I see.

32:24.640 --> 32:28.960
It is assigning probabilities to different pre-programmed rules of enlargement.

32:30.000 --> 32:36.960
True operator A requires the system to face a mismatch that falls entirely outside of all pre-programmed priors,

32:37.420 --> 32:42.700
and to respond by genuinely creating a boundary condition that its designer never anticipated.

32:43.280 --> 32:45.180
Let me see if I can translate that back to the restaurant.

32:45.300 --> 32:47.340
It's like having a physical menu that says at the bottom,

32:47.460 --> 32:50.380
if you don't like anything here, tell the chef and he will make you a surprise meal.

32:50.380 --> 32:55.820
When the chef makes the surprise meal, the boundaries of the menu didn't actually change.

32:56.320 --> 32:58.800
The rule for the surprise was already printed on the paper.

32:58.900 --> 32:59.340
Exactly.

32:59.560 --> 33:05.260
True operator A would be if the customer got so frustrated with the food that they walked into the kitchen,

33:05.600 --> 33:09.500
fired the chef, tore down the walls, and turned the restaurant into a shoe store.

33:09.500 --> 33:11.880
That captures the spirit of it perfectly.

33:12.080 --> 33:12.220
Yeah.

33:12.500 --> 33:15.280
The Bayesian models have the surprise rule printed on the menu.

33:15.420 --> 33:15.600
Right.

33:15.880 --> 33:18.980
True domain revision is changing the fundamental nature of the establishment.

33:19.240 --> 33:22.320
I think that dismantles the Bayesian objection beautifully,

33:22.600 --> 33:27.020
but there is a second objection that feels much more philosophically dangerous.

33:27.540 --> 33:28.880
The infinite regress objection.

33:28.880 --> 33:29.320
Yes.

33:29.520 --> 33:33.000
The critic says, okay, you have this concept of meta-admissibility.

33:33.880 --> 33:39.360
If the space of beliefs, omega, can be wrong, and it requires operator A to fix it,

33:39.460 --> 33:42.000
well, operator A is governed by its own set of rules.

33:42.380 --> 33:43.920
The paper calls these rules D.

33:44.280 --> 33:45.460
What if D is wrong?

33:45.660 --> 33:48.520
Ah, the classic turtles all the way down problem.

33:49.200 --> 33:49.680
Exactly.

33:49.900 --> 33:52.060
If the rules for changing the space can be wrong,

33:52.540 --> 33:55.740
don't you need another higher operator to fix those rules?

33:55.740 --> 33:58.000
And another operator to fix that one.

33:58.520 --> 34:02.700
Doesn't this whole framework collapse under an infinite loop of meta-revisions?

34:03.580 --> 34:06.100
It is a trap that trips up a lot of cognitive theories.

34:06.420 --> 34:09.700
But Flyxion resolves it using a concept from formal logic.

34:10.520 --> 34:12.780
Acknowledging that there is a higher level of rules,

34:13.360 --> 34:17.200
what the paper formally writes as gamma mapping to gamma prime,

34:17.460 --> 34:19.880
is not the same as a vicious regress.

34:20.140 --> 34:21.140
I'm not a logician.

34:21.240 --> 34:22.180
Why doesn't it go up forever?

34:22.180 --> 34:27.220
Because a system can operate perfectly, logically, and completely at one level

34:27.220 --> 34:30.300
while remaining entirely silent on the next level up.

34:30.900 --> 34:35.520
You do not need a grand, unified theory of everything to have a functional theory of something.

34:35.920 --> 34:36.180
Right.

34:36.620 --> 34:38.440
Flyxion compares it to first-order logic.

34:38.920 --> 34:42.420
You can build perfectly sound mathematical arguments using first-order logic

34:42.420 --> 34:46.180
without ever needing to invoke the complex machinery of second-order quantification.

34:46.920 --> 34:49.600
So you don't need to explain how the laws of the universe change,

34:49.600 --> 34:53.520
nor to explain how a human changes their mind about a shadow in a dark room.

34:53.840 --> 34:54.100
Right.

34:54.540 --> 34:59.340
The paper only needs U, Q, P, and A to be distinct operators.

34:59.780 --> 35:02.900
It does not need to solve the absolute foundation of reality

35:02.900 --> 35:07.000
to prove that those four mechanics are mathematically different from each other.

35:07.360 --> 35:10.640
They function distinctly at their designated level of the hierarchy.

35:10.640 --> 35:15.520
It's like saying you don't need to understand quantum mechanics to successfully play a game of baseball.

35:15.760 --> 35:19.880
The rules of baseball operate just fine at their own level of reality.

35:19.960 --> 35:20.440
Exactly.

35:21.060 --> 35:23.560
Now there is one final caveat we should address.

35:24.800 --> 35:29.360
The most loyal defenders of predictive coding will point out that in the real world,

35:29.760 --> 35:33.060
generative models can sometimes be replaced or extended in practice.

35:33.520 --> 35:35.300
They will say, look at this AI we built.

35:35.520 --> 35:36.740
It adapted its structure.

35:37.040 --> 35:38.560
And what is Flyxion's response to that?

35:38.560 --> 35:41.840
The response is essentially, I completely agree with you.

35:41.920 --> 35:42.440
Wait, really?

35:42.640 --> 35:42.820
Yeah.

35:43.240 --> 35:46.300
Flyxion is not claiming that active inference or predictive systems

35:46.300 --> 35:49.080
are literally incapable of structural adaptation.

35:49.780 --> 35:55.260
The argument is that if a generative model does replace its structure in response to a mismatch,

35:55.540 --> 35:58.460
it has simply acquired an implementation of operator A.

35:58.560 --> 35:59.140
Oh, I see.

35:59.360 --> 36:02.540
It did not achieve that revision using only precision weighting.

36:03.200 --> 36:05.720
It had to add a new tool to its toolbox.

36:05.720 --> 36:09.900
Flyxion has taxonomy is categorizing that tool.

36:10.240 --> 36:12.100
It is not disputing its existence.

36:12.880 --> 36:18.380
The plea is simply for researchers to acknowledge that a saw is functionally different from a hammer,

36:18.720 --> 36:20.620
even if you keep them both in the same toolbox.

36:20.840 --> 36:25.160
You can't just call every tool a hammer just because you bought them all at the predictive coding hardware store.

36:25.160 --> 36:30.140
If you're using a saw to cut the boundaries of the space, you have to admit you are using a saw.

36:30.380 --> 36:32.520
That is the absolute crux of the debate.

36:32.720 --> 36:36.220
This has been a deeply theoretical, mathematically heavy exploration,

36:36.500 --> 36:41.080
but I want to make sure we tie this directly back to the listeners' daily intellectual life.

36:41.400 --> 36:46.480
We've gone through the topologies, the operators, the dark rooms, the infinite tables at the Chinese restaurant.

36:46.480 --> 36:52.760
If you are listening to this on your commute or while doing the dishes, what is the ultimate takeaway?

36:53.200 --> 36:59.480
The profound takeaway is that there are two entirely distinct ways a system and a human being can be wrong.

36:59.860 --> 37:03.500
You can occupy the wrong position in your space of possibilities.

37:03.500 --> 37:08.620
That is an ordinary error, and it is what standard inference and learning are designed to correct.

37:09.040 --> 37:13.020
Or you can be operating with the wrong space of possibilities entirely.

37:13.340 --> 37:17.620
That is an error about what inference is even permitted to evaluate.

37:17.880 --> 37:20.140
The wrong position versus the wrong space.

37:20.360 --> 37:25.200
It is such a clean, powerful distinction, and it is incredibly applicable.

37:25.400 --> 37:30.740
If you are listening to this, I really encourage you to apply this diagnostic to your own life this week.

37:30.740 --> 37:33.980
Think about an intractable mismatch you were facing.

37:34.420 --> 37:38.720
Maybe it is a stubborn problem at work that you have been grinding on for months.

37:39.120 --> 37:43.320
Maybe it is a recurring argument in a relationship that you just cannot seem to resolve,

37:43.420 --> 37:45.940
no matter how much you talk it out or gather new evidence.

37:45.980 --> 37:47.280
Right. More data doesn't help.

37:47.360 --> 37:51.720
Exactly. Ask yourself, am I just trying to turn down the precision weight of this error?

37:51.960 --> 37:54.500
Am I just shifting my guesses around on the existing menu?

37:54.740 --> 37:58.460
Or do I need to fundamentally refuse the boundaries of the problem I have constructed?

37:58.460 --> 38:03.120
It requires a profound, almost uncomfortable level of self-awareness

38:03.120 --> 38:06.600
to recognize when you do not need more evidence,

38:06.960 --> 38:11.960
but rather need to completely rewrite the rules of what you consider admissible.

38:12.180 --> 38:12.460
Yeah.

38:12.700 --> 38:14.960
Are you arguing over which puzzle piece fits

38:14.960 --> 38:17.780
when you should be asking if the piece even belongs in the box?

38:18.120 --> 38:19.560
The piece is from the wrong game.

38:19.740 --> 38:21.740
It brings us right back to the kitchen table.

38:22.000 --> 38:23.820
But before we wrap up this deep dive,

38:23.920 --> 38:27.040
there is one final lingering question I want to leave you with.

38:27.040 --> 38:31.240
It is inspired by a tiny footnote buried in the Solst material,

38:31.740 --> 38:36.100
specifically a reference to another work titled Boundaries Are Computational Objects.

38:36.140 --> 38:37.140
That's a great paper, too.

38:37.520 --> 38:41.700
Right. Because Flickarian hints that this framework isn't just about abstract cognitive thought.

38:42.200 --> 38:45.680
If boundaries aren't just passive statistical screens, if they're active,

38:46.180 --> 38:49.980
computational objects constantly deciding what is admissible and what is refused.

38:49.980 --> 38:52.700
What does this mean for the physical world?

38:53.360 --> 38:57.180
It expands the scope of the theory into biology, sociology, and beyond.

38:57.680 --> 39:01.120
Right. What does this mean for the biological boundaries of our cells?

39:01.580 --> 39:04.380
What about the social boundaries of our human networks?

39:04.540 --> 39:09.220
Are the physical membranes of our cells running this exact same hidden machinery of refusal,

39:09.880 --> 39:12.740
calculating what molecules are allowed to exist within them

39:12.740 --> 39:16.140
and what is fundamentally deleted from their biological reality?

39:16.140 --> 39:17.200
It's wild to think about.

39:17.520 --> 39:22.820
Are algorithms, our social media feeds, our societies constantly running Operator A in the background?

39:22.940 --> 39:23.900
It makes you wonder.

39:24.300 --> 39:28.000
As you go through your day looking at the choices and the possibilities in front of you,

39:28.380 --> 39:32.900
what unseen items has your own cognitive architecture actively refused?

39:33.580 --> 39:35.860
What realities are you completely blind to

39:35.860 --> 39:40.060
simply because your brain's boundary mechanisms never even let them on the menu?

39:40.060 --> 39:44.580
It's a staggering thought. It challenges us to look not just at what we see,

39:44.760 --> 39:48.720
but at the hidden mechanisms that determine what we are even capable of seeing.

39:49.020 --> 39:52.520
A puzzle where you can't even see the pieces your brain has actively rejected,

39:52.720 --> 39:54.980
that is something to mull over as you stare at the table.

39:55.980 --> 39:58.500
Until next time, thanks for taking this deep dive with us.

